---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Hero from '../components/Hero.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="b-ark-chrome User Guide · b-oss"
  description="Install, connect, and back up your Blipfoto journal with b-ark-chrome — the b-oss backup extension for Chrome and Edge."
  nav="b-ark"
  narrow
  lightbox
>
  <Hero label="Guide hero" title="b-ark-chrome user guide">Install the extension, connect your account and run your first backup.</Hero>

  <section
    class="block"
    style="border-top: none; padding-top: 0"
    data-screen-label="Guide body"
  >
    <nav class="toc" aria-label="Contents">
      <p class="toc-title">Contents</p>
      <ol>
        <li><a href="#install">Install from the Chrome Web Store</a></li>
        <li><a href="#open">Open the backup page</a></li>
        <li><a href="#connect">Connect your Blipfoto account</a></li>
        <li><a href="#choose-folder">Choose a backup folder</a></li>
        <li><a href="#first-backup">Run your first backup</a></li>
        <li><a href="#chip">The status chip</a></li>
        <li><a href="#settings">Settings</a></li>
        <li><a href="#b-view">Browsing your journal with b-view</a></li>
        <li><a href="#keyboard">Keyboard</a></li>
        <li><a href="#ai">Using your backup with AI</a></li>
        <li><a href="#privacy">Privacy</a></li>
      </ol>
    </nav>

    <div class="prose">
      <h2 id="install">1. Install from the Chrome Web Store</h2>
      <p>
        b-ark-chrome installs in one click from the Chrome Web Store, and your browser keeps it up
        to date automatically. It works in <strong>Chrome or Edge</strong> on Windows, Mac
        and Linux.
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>Open the <a href="https://chromewebstore.google.com/detail/b-ark-chrome/apcmndghbieclompadigikblmlafccpe">b-ark-chrome listing on the Chrome Web Store</a>.</li>
        <li>Click <strong>"Add to Chrome"</strong>, then confirm <strong>"Add extension"</strong>.</li>
        <li>
          Click the <strong>puzzle-piece (Extensions)</strong> icon in the toolbar and
          <strong>pin</strong> b-ark-chrome so it stays visible.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <figure>
        <img src="assets/guide-chrome/install.png" alt="Installing b-ark-chrome from the Chrome Web Store" />
        <figcaption>Adding b-ark-chrome from the Chrome Web Store</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h2 id="open">2. Open the backup page</h2>
      <p>
        Click the <strong>b-ark-chrome icon</strong> in your toolbar to open the backup
        page. This is the one tab where you connect your account, choose a folder and
        watch backups run. Clicking the icon again brings you back to it rather than
        opening a second one.
      </p>
      <figure>
        <img src="assets/guide-chrome/backup-page.png" alt="The b-ark-chrome backup page" />
        <figcaption>The b-ark-chrome backup page</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h2 id="connect">3. Connect your Blipfoto account</h2>
      <p>
        b-ark-chrome backs up a <strong>single Blipfoto account</strong>. To connect it,
        you give it read-only access to your journal.
      </p>
      <figure>
        <img src="assets/guide-chrome/connect-account.png" alt="Connecting your Blipfoto account" />
        <figcaption>Connecting your Blipfoto account</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <ol>
        <li>Click <strong>"Sign in to Blipfoto"</strong>.</li>
        <li>
          A Blipfoto authorisation page opens. Log in if needed and approve
          <strong>read</strong> access.
        </li>
        <li>
          You are returned to the backup page, which asks you to choose a folder
          (next step).
        </li>
      </ol>
      <blockquote>
        <p>
          <strong>Your token stays on your machine.</strong> b-ark-chrome keeps your sign-in
          in your browser, encrypted, and uses it only to talk to Blipfoto. See <a href="#privacy">Privacy</a> below.
        </p>
      </blockquote>

      <h2 id="choose-folder">4. Choose a backup folder</h2>
      <p>
        After you sign in, b-ark-chrome asks you to pick a folder on your computer for your
        backup. Files are saved straight into it — nothing is uploaded anywhere.
      </p>
      <figure>
        <img src="assets/guide-chrome/choose-folder.png" alt="Choosing a backup folder" />
        <figcaption>Choosing a backup folder</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <ol>
        <li>Click <strong>"Choose backup folder"</strong>.</li>
        <li>Select an existing empty folder, or create a new one, then confirm.</li>
        <li>
          When the browser asks, allow b-ark-chrome to save files in that folder.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <blockquote>
        <p>
          <strong>Re-granting permission:</strong> the browser may ask you to confirm folder
          access again after a restart. If the backup page says folder access needs
          renewing, click <strong>"Grant folder access"</strong> and approve. Your folder
          choice is remembered.
        </p>
      </blockquote>
      <p>
        If the folder already holds a backup made by b-ark-chrome (for example on another
        computer), your earlier settings are picked up automatically. Don't point
        b-ark-chrome and b-ark at the same folder.
      </p>

      <h2 id="first-backup">5. Run your first backup</h2>
      <p>
        Click <strong>"Back up now"</strong> on the backup page. After that,
        b-ark-chrome keeps your archive up to date by itself (both options can be switched
        in <a href="#settings">Settings</a>):
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Backup on visit.</strong> Each time you open a Blipfoto page,
          b-ark-chrome checks whether a backup is due (daily or weekly, as you choose) and,
          if so, runs one quietly in the background.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Backup on publish.</strong> Optional, and off by default. When it's on and you
          publish or save an entry on Blipfoto, b-ark-chrome captures it within seconds.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <blockquote>
        <p>
          <strong>When backups run.</strong> Backups are triggered when you visit Blipfoto, when you publish if
          Backup on publish is on, or when you press "Back up now" — there is no background timer. If you
          post from a phone or tablet, the backup runs the next time you open Blipfoto in
          this browser.
        </p>
      </blockquote>
      <figure>
        <img src="assets/guide-chrome/backup-progress.png" alt="Backup in progress" />
        <figcaption>Backup in progress</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p>While backing up, a progress banner shows which phase b-ark-chrome is in:</p>

      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Phase</th>
            <th>What it means</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>NEW</td>
            <td>Fetching entries b-ark-chrome has never downloaded before.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>GAPS</td>
            <td>Filling in any entries that were missed on previous runs.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>REDO</td>
            <td>
              Re-downloading your most recent entries in case they have changed.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>FIX</td>
            <td>Repairing any images that failed to download cleanly on a previous run.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <p>
        If Blipfoto's rate limit is reached mid-backup, b-ark-chrome pauses and counts
        down, then carries on. Your journal thumbnails appear on the backup page as it
        goes. If a notice appears afterwards, it says what happened and offers buttons such
        as <strong>Retry</strong>, <strong>Sign in</strong> or <strong>Dismiss</strong>.
      </p>

      <h2 id="chip">6. The status chip</h2>
      <p>
        On Blipfoto pages, b-ark-chrome shows a small, draggable
        <strong>status chip</strong>. Its colour tells you the health of your backup at a
        glance (green = up to date, amber = incomplete or paused, red = needs attention), and it
        shows progress while a backup is running.
      </p>
      <figure>
        <img src="assets/guide-chrome/status-chip.png" alt="The b-ark-chrome status chip" />
        <figcaption>The draggable status chip on a Blipfoto page</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Drag</strong> the chip anywhere on the page; it remembers its position.</li>
        <li>
          <strong>Hover</strong> to see when you were last backed up, or what needs
          fixing.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Double-click</strong> it to open the backup page.
        </li>
        <li>
          You can hide the chip, or show your Blipfoto avatar on it, in
          <a href="#settings">Settings</a>. Backups still run when it is hidden.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="settings">7. Settings</h2>
      <p>
        Click the <strong>gear icon</strong> ("Open settings") on the backup page. Here you
        can change the backup folder, switch backup on visit and backup on publish on or
        off, hide the chip, sign in again, or sign out. The <strong>log</strong> icon
        beside it shows what b-ark-chrome has been doing.
      </p>
      <figure>
        <img src="assets/guide-chrome/settings-panel.png" alt="b-ark-chrome settings panel" />
        <figcaption>The settings panel</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h2 id="b-view">8. Browsing your journal with b-view</h2>
      <p>
        You can browse your backed-up journal with <strong>b-view</strong>, which has a
        thumbnail grid view and an entry view.
      </p>
      <figure>
        <img src="assets/guide/b-view-grid.png" alt="b-view thumbnail grid" />
        <figcaption>b-view thumbnail grid</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Zoom controls</strong> (+ / &minus; / reset) adjust thumbnail size between
          30% and 200%.
        </li>
        <li><strong>Left and right arrow keys</strong> move between pages of thumbnails.</li>
        <li>
          The <strong>calendar icon</strong> opens a date picker so you can jump straight to
          a specific month and year.
        </li>
        <li>Click any thumbnail to open the full entry.</li>
      </ul>
      <figure>
        <img src="assets/guide/b-view-entry.png" alt="b-view entry detail" />
        <figcaption>b-view entry detail</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p>
        Inside an entry you can see the full-size photo, title, description, tags, comments
        (including nested replies), engagement counts (views, stars, favourites), location,
        and camera EXIF data if present.
      </p>

      <h3>b-view direct (without the extension)</h3>
      <p>
        Your backup folder contains an <code>index.html</code> file. Open it in Chrome,
        Edge or Firefox.
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          b-view prompts you to <strong>"Open backup folder"</strong>: click it and select
          your backup folder using the native folder picker.
        </li>
        <li>Navigation is then identical to the grid and entry views above.</li>
      </ol>
      <blockquote>
        <p>
          <strong>Note:</strong> This mode requires the File System Access API, which is
          supported in Chrome, Edge and Firefox. Safari and older browsers are not
          supported.
        </p>
      </blockquote>

      <h3>b-view for publishing a website</h3>
      <p>
        b-view can publish your backup as a website too. Copy your backup folder to any
        static web host and browse to the URL — b-view does the rest. No setup required.
      </p>

      <h2 id="keyboard">9. Keyboard</h2>
      <p>
        You can get around your backed-up journal on the backup page using the keyboard.
        The keys do nothing while you are typing in a box, such as the search box.
      </p>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Key</th>
            <th>What it does</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Left / Right arrow</td>
            <td>In the thumbnail grid, go to the previous or next page.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Left / Right arrow</td>
            <td>On an entry, go to the older or newer entry.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Left / Right arrow</td>
            <td>With a photo enlarged, show the previous or next photo.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Ctrl and + / &minus; / 0</td>
            <td>Make the thumbnails bigger, smaller, or back to normal. With a photo enlarged, zoom the photo instead. Ctrl and the mouse wheel does the same.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Backspace</td>
            <td>On an entry, go back to the grid. With a photo enlarged, close it.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Esc</td>
            <td>Close the date picker or an open menu.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Tab, Enter</td>
            <td>Move between thumbnails and buttons (a ring shows where you are) and open or press the one you are on.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h2 id="ai">10. Using your backup with AI</h2>
      <p>
        Your backup folder contains a <code>README.md</code> file. It explains your
        journal's data format and gives guidance on using the backup with AI assistants:
        Claude, ChatGPT, Copilot, Gemini, and others.
      </p>
      <p><strong>The README includes:</strong></p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Ready-to-use example prompts (<em>"Create a PDF of all entries from 2022"</em>,
          <em>"Find my 10 happiest-sounding entries"</em>,
          <em>"What cameras have I used over the years?"</em>).
        </li>
        <li>
          Instructions for the AI on how to approach large journals (a two-pass method:
          score and shortlist all entries first, then produce the requested output).
        </li>
        <li>The full data schema so the AI understands the file and folder structure.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Recommended workflow using Claude Projects</h3>
      <ol>
        <li>Create a new <a href="https://claude.ai">Claude Project</a>.</li>
        <li>Upload your backup folder, or the files within it.</li>
        <li>
          Start your first message with:
          <em>"Please read the README.md file in this backup before we start."</em>
        </li>
        <li>
          Describe what you want: a photo book, a timeline, a search, a summary, or
          something else.
        </li>
      </ol>

      <h3>Example prompts</h3>
      <p>
        Here are some examples of things you could ask an AI to do with your backup. Feel
        free to adapt them.
      </p>

      <p><strong>By date</strong></p>
      <ul>
        <li><em>"Create a PDF document of all my entries from 2022."</em></li>
        <li><em>"Make a presentation of my entries between 1st and 31st August 2019."</em></li>
      </ul>

      <p><strong>By tag or keyword</strong></p>
      <ul>
        <li><em>"Build a photo book of all my entries tagged 'walking'."</em></li>
        <li><em>"Create a presentation of entries where I mention my dog."</em></li>
      </ul>

      <p><strong>By engagement</strong></p>
      <ul>
        <li><em>"Make a presentation of my 20 most favourited entries."</em></li>
        <li>
          <em
            >"Create a spreadsheet showing how many entries I posted each month, and which
            got the most views."</em
          >
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p><strong>By mood or theme</strong></p>
      <ul>
        <li><em>"Find my 10 happiest-sounding entries and make a presentation."</em></li>
        <li>
          <em
            >"Create a document of entries where I seem to be reflecting on getting
            older."</em
          >
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p><strong>By camera or location</strong></p>
      <ul>
        <li><em>"Make a photo book of everything I shot on my Fujifilm X100."</em></li>
        <li><em>"Create a presentation of all my entries taken in Japan."</em></li>
      </ul>

      <p><strong>Questions and searches</strong></p>
      <p>
        Not every request needs to produce a document. Sometimes you just want an answer.
        Try:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><em>"What are the dates of all my entries that mention Brighton?"</em></li>
        <li><em>"Where did I buy the loganberry jam I mentioned?"</em></li>
        <li>
          <em>"How many entries did I post in 2018, and which month was most active?"</em>
        </li>
        <li><em>"What cameras have I used over the years?"</em></li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="privacy">11. Privacy</h2>
      <p>
        b-ark-chrome backs up your journal to a folder <strong>you</strong> choose, on your
        own machine. Your Blipfoto access token is encrypted at rest in your browser and
        used only to talk to Blipfoto — it is never broadcast or sent anywhere else. The
        extension collects no analytics.
      </p>
      <p>
        For the full details of what data the extension handles and where it is stored, see
        the <a href="b-ark-chrome-privacy">b-ark-chrome privacy policy</a>.
      </p>
    </div>

    <p style="margin-top: 40px">
      <a class="btn" href="b-ark">&larr; Back to b-ark</a>
    </p>
  </section>
</Layout>
